artifact :the_pressure_schedule do
  name "The Pressure Schedule"
  aka "Admission 41806", "压力管道验收表"
  tags :legibility, :trade, :resistance

  prominence :marginal

  prose <<~PROSE
    A mineral-loaded slab standing on edge in bay nine of the #{ref :juyan_register_house, "Juyan Register House"}, admitted in 2071 out of a Lanzhou copying house's load. It holds the 2049 Chinese edition of a North American acceptance schedule for building chilled-water and brine piping: pipe classes by working pressure, joint preparation for each, the test at one and a half times the working figure, the ten minutes it must be held, and the requirement that every joint stand uncovered and be watched through the hold.

    It was queued on admission and read for the first time fourteen years later, when an examiner at #{ref :race_street_warehouse, "the Race Street warehouse"} requested it. In coalition territory the schedule survives as a citation in inspection records whose text migrated between systems and did not arrive.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :description, heading: "Six Faces And What Is On Them"
    Slab ware carries its relief deep and close-packed, so the slab came in with six countable faces where a porcelain piece of the same mass would have had three. #{ref :the_squeeze, "A reading"} takes a tenth of a millimetre off it to stand the pattern clean, and a badly struck brush takes the face and the two beneath it. Two readers at Juyan hold to the class and neither will take a slab in a month when the paper is short.

    What sits on a given face is not known until the face is lapped. The pattern runs through the body in no order anybody has been able to predict from the outside, so a warrant buys a face and receives whatever text that face carries. The pressure tables came up on the first face. The joint-preparation tables came up on the third. The second gave two pages of an index to a document that was never admitted.

    Three faces are spent and three stand. The thickness of each reading is entered on the piece's line, with the album number of the sheet it produced, under #{ref :entry_into_the_register, "the register's"} ordinary hand.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :limits, heading: "A Modified Adoption"
    The head of the first table carries the mark 修改采用 — modified adoption — rather than 等同采用, so the edition departs from the foreign schedule it names in at least two figures, and the table that lists the departures sits on a face nobody has bought.

    An extract off this slab therefore gives a claim examiner the shape of the work and not a guaranteed number: that the line was filled, brought above its working pressure, held while somebody stood at the joints, and signed off by a witness who is named on the record. #{ref :the_preservationists, "Preservationist"} practice enters the mark on every certificate issued from the piece, and the Race Street identity desk has never returned an extract on account of it.
  PROSE

  log "2026-08-09 — The surviving text is a national adoption rather than the original, and it carries an adoption mark distinguishing an identical restatement from a modified one, taken from standards-body practice where an adopting country's edition is the authoritative text in that language and states on its first page how far it departs from the source. That decided what an extract can settle: the procedure and the witness, not the exact figure."
  log "2026-08-09 — Test pressure at one and a half times working pressure, a ten-minute hold, and joints left uncovered and inspected through the hold are the building-services piping code's leak test. The witnessed hold is what makes the figures useful to a claim examiner, because a person who stood at the joints remembers standing there and a person who bought the paper does not know there was a hold."
end
